e(\"target = 1\")\n        sns.distplot(trian[trian['target']==0][feature], ax=axes[2]).set_title(\"target = 0\")\n        sns.despine(left=True)\n        plt.tight_layout()\n\nplot_distribution()\n# trian.drop('target', axis=1, inplace=True)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-03-17T08:56:20.655420Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-03-17T08:56:20.655844Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-03-17T08:57:37.504543Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2025-03-17T08:56:20.655806Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-03-17T08:57:37.503249Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### A few noteworthy  cols\n- EngineVersion': Version of the Antivirus Engine\n- 'AppVersion' : Version of the Antivirus Application\n- 'OSVersion' : Operating System Version\n- 'OSBuildNumber' : OS Build Number\n- 'OSBuildNumberOnly'\n- 'OSBuildRevisionOnly'","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Insights from the data\n1. its a huge data in terms of no. of columns, and there were redudandant columns as well, which were removed from the start.\n2. majority of the dataset was complete, less than 5% of the data was missing so simply just dropped the data, while filled them with mode in the test set\n3. majority of the columns were numerical, but there were more no of categorical cols than binary, so had to dorp the cols who had huge no of categories to avoid expanding the dataset while using one-hot encoder.\n4. the target varible is balanced i.e there is no class imabalance in the data.\n5. the hypothesis i framed was that older OS and anti-virus versions would be more prone to the new types of malware, but from the EDA, we see that:\n    1. there is no data available for the older versions of the anti-virus (the engine verion col), and the graph also suggest that particularly the version 1.1.15100.1 was very faulty\n    2. while the same problem of no data holds for app version of the anti-virus, the most recent version was particularly fault